\begin{abstract}
Execution traces are increasingly used to enhance code language models, but which trace components matter for downstream tasks remains poorly understood.
We present the first systematic component ablation of trace representations for \emph{verdict prediction}---classifying whether a test passes or fails---across four code-specialized models spanning 1.5B--13B parameters and three architecture families (Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, Qwen-2.5-Coder-1.5B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, and CodeLlama-13B).
Our key finding is that exception events alone achieve $\geq$99.33\% accuracy on all four models regardless of scale, while the no-trace baseline exhibits strict scale monotonicity (67.31\%--76.50\%), demonstrating that information quality dominates model scale for verdict prediction.
We decompose the gap between full traces and compressed representations into gradient dilution (26\%) and information overload (74\%), establishing that irrelevant trace content---not training signal quality---is the primary bottleneck.
Component ablation reveals a two-tier diagnostic hierarchy: Tier~1 signals (exception events, assertion points) individually achieve ${\geq}$99.8\% accuracy, while Tier~2 signals (loop compression) reach only 85.6\%.
These results reframe verdict prediction as fault detection, where the minimal sufficient signal is a compact exception event that transfers across model families and scales without requiring deep trace reasoning.
\end{abstract}

